1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,273
Welcome to the Modern Java Enterprise Handbook.

2
00:00:04,273 --> 00:00:07,320
This is chapter forty: the HTTP client.

3
00:00:07,770 --> 00:00:13,930
Java 11 standardised a real HTTP client, and by Java 26 it speaks HTTP/3.

4
00:00:13,930 --> 00:00:30,469
Two things have changed since: virtual threads made the simple synchronous call the right default again, and HTTP/2 multiplexing means connection pool sizing no longer works the way people assume.

5
00:00:31,369 --> 00:00:36,152
Here's HttpURLConnection, designed around 1997 assumptions.

6
00:00:36,152 --> 00:00:42,718
Set the method, enable output, set two timeouts, write the body, read the status.

7
00:00:42,718 --> 00:00:51,635
Then errors come back on a different stream from successes, so forgetting that ternary loses every error body.

8
00:00:52,085 --> 00:00:57,201
It's mutable, not thread-safe, with no HTTP/2 and no async.

9
00:00:57,201 --> 00:01:10,901
So most teams added OkHttp or Apache HttpClient, along with a dependency tree and its own vulnerability feed, for something the platform should have provided.

10
00:01:11,801 --> 00:01:13,711
There are a few objects.

11
00:01:13,711 --> 00:01:17,452
The HttpClient lives for the whole application.

12
00:01:17,452 --> 00:01:22,068
It owns the connections, the executor and the SSL context.

13
00:01:22,068 --> 00:01:23,023
One per app.

14
00:01:23,473 --> 00:01:27,078
An HttpRequest is per call, and immutable.

15
00:01:27,528 --> 00:01:30,323
A BodyPublisher supplies the request body.

16
00:01:30,773 --> 00:01:36,447
A BodyHandler decides how the response is read: as a string, a stream, a file.

17
00:01:36,897 --> 00:01:41,271
And the HttpResponse holds the status, headers and body.

18
00:01:41,721 --> 00:01:43,402
The asymmetry matters.

19
00:01:43,402 --> 00:01:49,056
The client is expensive and shared, everything else is cheap and per call.

20
00:01:49,056 --> 00:01:55,246
Creating a client per request leaks connection pools and defeats HTTP/2 entirely.

21
00:01:56,146 --> 00:01:59,573
What does the protocol version change?

22
00:01:59,573 --> 00:02:07,149
HTTP/1.1 carries one request at a time per connection, so you pool many connections.

23
00:02:07,149 --> 00:02:17,882
HTTP/2 multiplexes many streams over one TCP connection, though a lost packet still blocks everything at the TCP layer.

24
00:02:17,882 --> 00:02:28,885
HTTP/3 runs over QUIC on UDP, with fully independent streams, faster setup, and connections that survive a network change.

25
00:02:28,885 --> 00:02:31,681
Java 26's client speaks HTTP/3.

26
00:02:32,131 --> 00:02:43,993
The consequence: under HTTP/2, a single connection carries hundreds of concurrent requests, so the pool hardly matters, and tuning its size does nothing.

27
00:02:44,893 --> 00:02:49,793
Here's a correctly configured client, with four things doing real work.

28
00:02:49,793 --> 00:02:53,106
It's static final, owning the pool and executor.

29
00:02:53,106 --> 00:03:00,836
It sets both timeouts: connectTimeout covers only connecting, and the request timeout covers the whole exchange.

30
00:03:00,836 --> 00:03:07,185
Setting only the first leaves a hung read waiting forever, the most common misconfiguration.

31
00:03:07,185 --> 00:03:11,741
It sets follow redirects explicitly, because the default is never.

32
00:03:11,741 --> 00:03:18,849
And it checks the status code, because a five hundred is a perfectly successful send, not an exception.

33
00:03:19,749 --> 00:03:23,168
There's no built-in retry, so write it carefully.

34
00:03:23,168 --> 00:03:27,144
Retry connection failures and server errors with backoff.

35
00:03:27,144 --> 00:03:31,679
Never retry a four hundred, because the request itself was wrong.

36
00:03:31,679 --> 00:03:37,957
And only retry idempotent operations, which is why the request carries an idempotency key.

37
00:03:37,957 --> 00:03:41,445
Without one, a retried POST can create two orders.

38
00:03:41,895 --> 00:03:47,207
For large responses, don't use ofString, which buffers everything in memory.

39
00:03:47,207 --> 00:03:49,793
Stream with ofLines or ofInputStream.

40
00:03:49,793 --> 00:03:54,546
And close the stream, or the connection never goes back to the pool.

41
00:03:55,446 --> 00:03:56,567
Sync or async?

42
00:03:56,567 --> 00:04:04,171
Before Java 21, sendAsync with a chain of stages avoided blocking an expensive platform thread.

43
00:04:04,621 --> 00:04:09,976
On Java 21 and later, blocking on a virtual thread costs almost nothing.

44
00:04:09,976 --> 00:04:17,784
So the plain send call is the right default again, with real stack traces, try-catch, and ordinary loops.

45
00:04:18,234 --> 00:04:22,031
Keep sendAsync for pipelines that are genuinely event-driven.

46
00:04:22,931 --> 00:04:24,251
Production rules.

47
00:04:24,251 --> 00:04:26,113
One static final client.

48
00:04:26,113 --> 00:04:27,510
Set both timeouts.

49
00:04:27,510 --> 00:04:29,994
Set follow redirects explicitly.

50
00:04:29,994 --> 00:04:33,099
Check the status code on every response.

51
00:04:33,099 --> 00:04:36,125
Put idempotency keys on retried writes.

52
00:04:36,125 --> 00:04:39,230
And stream large bodies, and close them.

53
00:04:39,680 --> 00:04:42,501
Never create a client per request.

54
00:04:42,501 --> 00:04:45,405
Never assume a five hundred throws.

55
00:04:45,405 --> 00:04:49,803
Never retry a four hundred, or a non-idempotent POST.

56
00:04:49,803 --> 00:04:53,786
Never use ofString for multi-gigabyte responses.

57
00:04:53,786 --> 00:04:57,685
And don't bother tuning pool size under HTTP/2.

58
00:04:58,585 --> 00:04:59,327
To recap.

59
00:04:59,327 --> 00:05:03,942
One shared client, two timeouts, and explicit redirects.

60
00:05:03,942 --> 00:05:07,981
Error status codes are responses, not exceptions.

61
00:05:07,981 --> 00:05:11,689
Retry only idempotent requests, with backoff.

62
00:05:11,689 --> 00:05:16,716
HTTP/2 multiplexes, and HTTP/3 removes head-of-line blocking.

63
00:05:16,716 --> 00:05:22,073
And on virtual threads, the plain send call is the default again.

64
00:05:22,523 --> 00:05:24,750
That completes the runtime section.

65
00:05:24,750 --> 00:05:26,022
Thanks for watching.
